quations, given a smooth initial condition u₀(x) = u(x, 0), such that:\n\n1. The solution exists for all time t ≥ 0.\n2. The solution is smooth, i.e., u and p have continuous derivatives of all orders.\n\n**Partial Results**\n\nWhile a complete proof remains elusive, several partial results have been obtained:\n\n1. **Local existence and uniqueness**: For a given smooth initial condition, there exists a unique smooth solution to the Navier-Stokes Equations on a finite time interval [0, T), where T depends on the initial condition.\n2. **Global existence for small data**: For initial conditions that are sufficiently small in a certain norm, there exists a global smooth solution to the Navier-Stokes Equations.\n3. **Global existence for two-dimensional flows**: In two dimensions, the Navier-Stokes Equations have a global smooth solution for any smooth initial condition.\n\n**Challenges and Open Questions**\n\nThe main challenges in resolving the Navier-Stokes existence and smoothness problem are:\n\n1. **Loss of regularity**: The Navier-Stokes Equations are nonlinear, and the nonlinear term can cause a loss of regularity in the solution, making it difficult to prove global existence and smoothness.\n2. **Blow-up**: There is a possibility that the solution may \"blow up\" in finite time, i.e., the solution or its derivatives may become infinite.\n\nTo resolve the Navier-Stokes existence and smoothness problem, one would need to either:\n\n1. **Prove global existence and smoothness**: Show that, for any smooth initial condition, there exists a global smooth solution to the Navier-Stokes Equations.\n2. **Construct a counterexample**: Find a smooth initial condition for which the solution to the Navier-Stokes Equations blows up in finite time.\n\nUnfortunately, I don't have a complete proof to provide, as the problem remains unsolved. Researchers continue to work on this problem, and significant progress has been made in recent years. However, a definitive resolution rema